# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from vals import *

H1("3. Modelling")
FIG("Figure_1.png", "Problem set-up and workflow. (a) Layered slab with the pulse-train source at x = 0. (b) Decision variables of the pulse train. (c) Design loop. This figure is a schematic and contains no computed data.")
H2("3.1 Spatial discretisation")
P("After the quasi-static elimination of the displacement, the heat problem has a single unknown per node, which is the temperature increment, and the damage integral is evaluated as a post-processing quantity that is advanced together with the temperature. "
  "The weak form of the reduced energy equation Eq. (@red@) is obtained by multiplying by a test function $\\varphi$ that vanishes at $x=L$, integrating over the slab, and using the natural boundary condition at the adiabatic surface and the continuity of the flux at the interface,")
EQ("\\int_0^L\\varphi\\,\\mathcal{E}[\\theta]\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int_0^L\\partial_x\\varphi\\left(k\\,\\partial_x\\theta+k^{*}\\partial_xv\\right)\\mathrm{d}x=0", "weak")
P("where $\\mathcal{E}[\\theta]$ collects the time-derivative, perfusion and source terms of Eq. (@red@). "
  "The unknown is represented by continuous piecewise linear functions on a mesh with a node at the interface. The mesh has 16 elements of 0.25 mm in the tumour, 40 elements of 0.25 mm in the first 10 mm of the normal tissue, and 6 elements of 1 mm out to the clamped face, that is 62 elements in all. The element matrices for an element of length $\\ell_e$ in layer $i$ are")
EQ("\\left(\\mathbf{M}_e\\right)_{ab}=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{eff},i}\\ell_e}{6}\\left(1+\\delta_{ab}\\right),\\qquad\\left(\\mathbf{K}_e\\right)_{ab}=\\frac{k_i}{\\ell_e}\\left(2\\delta_{ab}-1\\right)", "elem")
P("(with $a,b\\in\\{1,2\\}$ the local node indices and $\\delta_{ab}$ the Kronecker symbol; the code stores the symmetric tridiagonal global matrices). The perfusion term is lumped, which keeps it positive and monotone for the nonlinear regulation function. "
  "The laser load vector is computed by three-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature of the product of the hat function and the profile $G(x)$ on each element, which resolves the optical depth of 2.6 mm with 0.25 mm elements. The lumped perfusion weight of a node is")
EQ("W_i=\\sum_{e:\\,i\\in e}\\omega_{0,e}\\,\\frac{\\ell_e}{2}", "lump")
P("with the sum over the elements that contain the node, so that the interface node carries a contribution from each layer.")
H2("3.2 Time discretisation")
P("All time derivatives are approximated by the second-order backward differentiation formula (BDF2) with constant step $\\Delta t$ and with zero history before $t=0$, which is consistent with the rest state Eq. (@ic@),")
EQ("D\\theta^n=\\frac{3\\theta^n-4\\theta^{n-1}+\\theta^{n-2}}{2\\,\\Delta t}", "bdf")
P("and the second derivative is the square of this operator, which is a five-term convolution,")
EQ("D^2\\theta^n=\\sum_{j=0}^{4}d^{(2)}_j\\,\\theta^{n-j},\\qquad\\sum_jd^{(2)}_jz^j=\\left[\\frac{3-4z+z^2}{2\\Delta t}\\right]^2", "bdf2")
P("The lag operators of Eq. (@red@) are applied to the full expression: the product $(1+\\tau_qD)$ acts on the sum of the storage, perfusion and source terms, and the perfusion product $\\omega\\theta$ and the source are stored at the previous two steps. With $\\tau=\\tau_q+\\tau_0$ and with $d_0=3/(2\\Delta t)$, the fully discrete system for the nodal vector $\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^n$ is")
EQ("\\left[\\left(d_0+\\tau d^{(2)}_0\\right)\\mathbf{M}+\\left(1+\\tau_qd_0\\right)\\mathbf{W}\\!\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^n\\right)+\\mathbf{K}+\\frac{\\Delta t}{2}\\mathbf{K}^{*}\\right]\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^n=\\mathbf{r}^n", "disc")
P("The right-hand side contains the history of the storage term, of the perfusion product $\\mathbf{W}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}$, the lagged source and the thermal displacement,")
EQ("\\mathbf{r}^n=-\\mathbf{M}\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\left(d_j+\\tau d^{(2)}_j\\right)\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n-j}-\\tau_q\\sum_{j=1}^{2}d_j\\,\\mathbf{W}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n-j}+\\mathbf{q}\\left[\\bar f_n+\\tau_qD\\bar f_n\\right]-\\mathbf{K}^{*}\\!\\left(\\mathbf{v}^{n-1}+\\frac{\\Delta t}{2}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n-1}\\right)", "rhs")
P("where $d_j$ are the coefficients of Eq. (@bdf@) and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the nodal load for unit intensity. The thermal displacement is updated with the trapezoidal rule, which makes the $k^*$ term implicit with the factor $\\Delta t/2$ used in Eq. (@disc@),")
EQ("\\mathbf{v}^{n}=\\mathbf{v}^{n-1}+\\frac{\\Delta t}{2}\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n}+\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n-1}\\right)", "vupd")
P("The pulse train is not sampled at the time levels. Its step average is used instead, so that the energy of every pulse is exact for arbitrary pulse widths that are not multiples of the step,")
EQ("\\bar f_n=\\frac{\\mathcal{F}(n\\Delta t)-\\mathcal{F}((n-1)\\Delta t)}{\\Delta t}", "fbar")
EQ("\\mathcal{F}(t)=\\int_0^tf\\,\\mathrm{d}s=k\\,t_p+\\min\\left(t-kP,\\,t_p\\right)\\quad\\text{for }k=\\min\\left(\\mathrm{floor}(t/P),N_p\\right)<N_p", "Fcum")
P("(with $\\mathcal{F}=N_pt_p$ for $k=N_p$). For the nonlinear regulation function the matrix $\\mathbf{W}$ depends on the unknown. In the optimization runs one linearised pass is used, in which $g_i$ is evaluated at the linear extrapolation of the two previous steps. For the verification runs the Picard iteration")
EQ("\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n,(m+1)}=\\left[\\mathbf{A}\\!\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n,(m)}\\right)\\right]^{-1}\\mathbf{r}^n,\\qquad\\max\\left|\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n,(m+1)}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{n,(m)}\\right|<10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{K}", "picard")
P("is run to convergence. The tridiagonal systems are solved with the Thomas algorithm. The Picard iteration needed on average " + f2(V["prod"]["iters_mean"]) + " passes per step and at most " + str(V["prod"]["iters_max"]) + ". "
  "The one-pass linearised scheme and the converged Picard iteration differ by at most " + sc(max(V["prod"]["picard"]), 1) + " in relative terms for the five design metrics (peak temperature, minimum tumour damage integral, interface damage integral, collateral integral and peak stress), so that the cheap variant is adequate for the search. "
  "The in-plane stress is evaluated at the nodes from Eq. (@sig@) with the BDF2 temperature rate,")
EQ("\\sigma^n_{\\parallel,i}=-s_i\\left(\\theta^n+\\tau_1D\\theta^n\\right)", "sigd")
H2("3.3 Quadrature of the burn integral")
P("The burn integral at each node is advanced over a step with the temperature taken as linear in the step and the rate integrated by composite Simpson's rule with $n_s=4$ panels,")
EQ("\\Delta\\Omega^n=\\frac{\\Delta t}{3n_s}\\left[k_d(\\theta_0)+4\\sum_{\\mathrm{odd}}k_d(\\theta_m)+2\\sum_{\\mathrm{even}}k_d(\\theta_m)+k_d(\\theta_{n_s})\\right]", "simpson")
P("with $\\theta_m=\\theta^{n-1}+(\\theta^n-\\theta^{n-1})m/n_s$. The error of this rule for a smooth rate function is")
EQ("\\left|\\Delta\\Omega-\\Delta\\Omega_{\\mathrm{exact}}\\right|\\le\\frac{\\Delta t^5}{180\\,n_s^4}\\max\\left|\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^4k_d}{\\mathrm{d}t^4}\\right|", "simperr")
P("and the burn integral is advanced only at nodes where the temperature increment exceeds 3 K, since the rate below this level is negligible within the horizon. The tumour integral is advanced at nodes in $x\\le h$ and the normal-tissue integral at nodes in $x\\ge h$, so the interface node carries both.")
H2("3.4 Laplace-domain reference solution")
P("An independent reference is obtained for the linear problem, in which the perfusion coefficient is held constant ($c_w=0$ and no shutdown) and inertia is retained. With the Laplace parameter $s$, the lag factors become $1+\\tau_qs$ and the time-derivative operator becomes $s(1+(\\tau_q+\\tau_0)s)$. "
  "In each layer, the energy equation and the momentum equation for the strain $e=\\partial_xu$ form the coupled system")
EQ("\\kappa_i\\hat\\theta''=\\left[\\rho c_vT_s+\\omega\\left(1+\\tau_qs\\right)\\right]\\hat\\theta+T_0\\beta T_s\\hat e-\\left(1+\\tau_qs\\right)I_0\\mu_aG(x)\\hat f(s),\\quad T_s=s\\left[1+(\\tau_q+\\tau_0)s\\right],\\quad\\kappa_i=k+\\frac{k^{*}}{s}", "lap")
P("and")
EQ("\\hat e''=\\frac{\\rho s^2}{(1+\\tau_1s)c_{11}}\\hat e+\\frac{\\beta}{c_{11}}\\hat\\theta''", "lap2")
P("Writing $\\mathbf{y}=(\\hat e,\\hat\\theta)^{\\mathrm{T}}$, the homogeneous system is $\\mathbf{y}''=\\mathbf{M}\\mathbf{y}$ with a $2\\times2$ matrix whose eigenvalues are the squares of the two decay constants of the thermal and the elastic modes,")
EQ("\\det\\mathbf{M}=m\\,a,\\qquad\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\mathbf{M}=m+\\frac{\\beta}{c_{11}}b+a,\\qquad m=\\frac{\\rho s^2}{(1+\\tau_1s)c_{11}}", "eig")
P("with $a=(\\rho c_vT_s+\\omega(1+\\tau_qs))/\\kappa$ and $b=T_0\\beta T_s/\\kappa$. The small eigenvalue is computed from the determinant divided by the large one to avoid cancellation, and the elastic mode, whose decay length is far longer than the slab, is expanded in hyperbolic functions to keep the $8\\times8$ boundary-matching system well conditioned. "
  "Each exponential of the Gardner source gives a particular solution")
EQ("\\mathbf{y}_p=\\mathbf{c}\\,e^{-\\gamma x},\\qquad\\left(\\gamma^2\\mathbf{I}-\\mathbf{M}\\right)\\mathbf{c}=-\\frac{(1+\\tau_qs)\\,I_0\\mu_a\\hat f(s)\\,B}{\\kappa}\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{c_{11}},\\,1\\right)^{\\mathrm{T}},\\quad\\gamma=\\frac{l}{\\delta}", "part")
P("where $B$ and $l$ stand for $B_1,l_1$ and $-B_2,l_2$. The transform of the pulse train is a geometric series,")
EQ("\\hat f(s)=\\frac{1-e^{-st_p}}{s}\\,\\frac{1-e^{-sN_pP}}{1-e^{-sP}}", "fhat")
P("The boundary and interface conditions, Eqs. (@bc0@) to (@interf@), give an $8\\times8$ complex linear system for the amplitudes of the four modes in each layer. The time-domain solution is recovered by the Durbin formula with the Fourier series trapezoidal sum,")
EQ("f(t)\\approx\\frac{e^{ct}}{T}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{Re}\\,\\hat F(c)+\\sum_{k=1}^{N}\\mathrm{Re}\\left\\{\\hat F\\!\\left(c+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}k\\pi}{T}\\right)e^{\\mathrm{i}k\\pi t/T}\\right\\}\\right],\\quad T=2t,\\;c=\\frac{6}{t}", "durbin")
P("with $N=2500$ terms, accelerated by the Wynn epsilon algorithm applied to the last 40 partial sums $S_k$,")
EQ("\\epsilon_{k+1}^{(j)}=\\epsilon_{k-1}^{(j+1)}+\\frac{1}{\\epsilon_k^{(j+1)}-\\epsilon_k^{(j)}},\\qquad\\epsilon_0^{(j)}=S_j", "wynn")
P("The difference between the accelerated sum and the plain sum is below " + pct(max(max(V["valid"][m]["trunc"]) for m in V["valid"]), 1) + " per cent of the peak temperature in every verification case, which is a measure of the inversion error.")
H2("3.5 Verification measures")
P("Besides the comparison with the Laplace reference, the code is checked by an energy balance. For the Pennes and MGL models, where the stored energy is $\\int C_{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\theta+\\tau_0\\partial_t\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, the balance of incident energy, stored energy, perfusion loss and boundary flux reads")
EQ("E_{\\mathrm{in}}=S(t_e)+\\int_0^{t_e}\\!\\int_0^L\\omega\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int_0^{t_e}k\\,\\partial_x\\theta(L,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\quad S=\\int_0^LC_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\left(\\theta+\\tau_0\\partial_t\\theta\\right)\\mathrm{d}x,\\quad E_{\\mathrm{in}}=N_pt_pI_0\\mu_a\\int_0^LG\\,\\mathrm{d}x", "energyb")
P("The observed convergence order and the relative error measure are")
EQ("p=\\log_2\\frac{e(\\Delta)}{e(\\Delta/2)},\\qquad e(\\Delta)=\\frac{\\max_k\\left|Y_k(\\Delta)-Y_k^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right|}{\\max_k\\left|Y_k^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right|}", "order")
P("where $Y$ is any monitored quantity and $\\Delta$ is the step or the mesh size.")
H2("3.6 Optimization algorithm")
P("The continuous design variables are transformed to an unconstrained vector $\\mathbf{z}=(z_1,z_2,z_3)$, so that the intensity and pulse width stay positive and the duty cycle stays in its bounds,")
EQ("I_0=e^{z_1},\\qquad t_p=e^{z_2},\\qquad\\frac{t_p}{P}=0.1+\\frac{0.9}{1+e^{-z_3}}", "repar")
P("The pulse number $N_p$ is an integer and is handled by an outer loop over $N_p\\in\\{1,2,3,4,6,8,12\\}$, which is trivially parallel. For each $N_p$ the search begins with 14 random screening evaluations, and Nelder–Mead is run from the best one or two of them. The Nelder–Mead step sequence is the reflection, expansion, contraction and shrink of the simplex of vertices $\\mathbf{z}_j$ around the centroid $\\mathbf{z}_c$ of all but the worst vertex,")
EQ("\\mathbf{z}_r=\\mathbf{z}_c+\\left(\\mathbf{z}_c-\\mathbf{z}_{n+1}\\right),\\quad\\mathbf{z}_e=\\mathbf{z}_c+2\\left(\\mathbf{z}_c-\\mathbf{z}_{n+1}\\right),\\quad\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathrm{con}}=\\mathbf{z}_c+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\mathbf{z}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{z}_c\\right)", "nm")
P("with at most 60 iterations and an initial simplex step of $(0.4,0.5,0.8)$. The constraints are treated by an exact-penalty function. The normalised violations of the kill, vaporization, stress and treatment-time constraints are")
EQ("v_1=\\frac{\\max\\left(0,\\ln\\Omega_c-\\ln\\min_x\\Omega_T\\right)}{\\ln\\Omega_c},\\quad v_2=\\frac{\\max\\left(0,T_{\\max}-T_{\\mathrm{vap}}\\right)}{20\\,\\mathrm{K}},\\quad v_3=\\frac{\\max\\left(0,\\sigma_{\\max}-\\sigma_{\\lim}\\right)}{\\sigma_{\\lim}},\\quad v_4=\\frac{\\max\\left(0,N_pP-t_{\\mathrm{treat,max}}\\right)}{t_{\\mathrm{treat,max}}}", "viol")
P("and the penalised objective that is minimised is")
EQ("\\Phi(\\mathbf{d})=\\min\\left(J,30\\right)+50\\sum_{k=1}^{4}v_k+200\\sum_{k=1}^{4}v_k^2", "pen")
P("During the search the model is run with $\\Delta t=0.4$ s, and the discretisation error of this setting against $\\Delta t=0.05$ s is below 0.3 per cent for the quantities of interest. The best design is then re-evaluated with $\\Delta t=0.05$ s, and a bisection on the intensity within $\\pm8$ per cent restores strict feasibility if the penalised optimum has a small residual violation. "
  "The Pareto front is constructed by the epsilon-constraint method, in which the guaranteed kill depth fraction $c$ in Eq. (@kill@) is stepped from 0.3 to 1 and the collateral objective is minimised for each value,")
EQ("\\min_{\\mathbf{d}}J_F(\\mathbf{d})\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\Omega_T\\ge\\Omega_c\\ \\text{on}\\ [0,ch]\\ \\text{and the other constraints},\\quad c\\in\\{0.3,0.4,\\ldots,1\\}", "eps")
P("The effect of the conduction model on the safe dose is measured with a dose multiplier. For a fixed pulse-train shape $(t_p,P,N_p)$, the multiplier $s^*$ is the smallest factor on the intensity for which the kill constraint is met with equality, found by bisection,")
EQ("s^{*}=\\min\\left\\{s>0:\\ \\min_{0\\le x\\le h}\\Omega_T\\!\\left(x,t_e;\\,sI_0\\right)\\ge\\Omega_c\\right\\}", "dose")
P("The kill constraint is monotone in the intensity, which justifies the bisection. All numerical results quoted below were computed with the code described here, using only the Python standard library.")

H2("3.7 Modelling choices and their justification")
P("Several choices in the model were made deliberately and deserve a short justification, because each can be questioned. The first is the elimination of the displacement. The alternative is to retain the inertia term and to solve for the displacement and the temperature together. This was avoided for a practical reason, which is that on a time scale of seconds the elastic waves oscillate with periods of the order of ten microseconds, so that any time step suited to the heat conduction would under-resolve them and would produce numerical noise that has nothing to do with the physics. "
  "The Laplace-domain reference retains the inertia exactly, so that the claim of negligible inertia does not rest on the reduced model alone but on a comparison with the unreduced one. The second choice is the use of the in-plane stress. The axial stress is zero in the quasi-static limit, and a safety limit on a quantity that is identically zero would be empty. The in-plane stress under lateral constraint is the standard measure of thermal stress for a heated layer that is bonded to a larger body, and it is the quantity that varies discontinuously across the interface because of the difference in stiffness and expansion between the tissues. "
  "The third choice is the placement of the temperature-rate lag in the MGL model. The lag $\\tau_0$ is applied to the storage and coupling terms and not to the perfusion sink or the source, which follows the structure of the modified Green–Lindsay energy equation of the source study, so that the MGL model is not simply a renamed Cattaneo–Vernotte model; in the CV model the lag acts on the whole energy balance, including the source.")
P("The fourth choice is the handling of the source at the time levels. A rectangular pulse is discontinuous, and the CV model differentiates the source in time. The step average of Eq. (@fbar@) makes the delivered energy exact, and the discrete derivative of the averaged source produces the correct impulse in the heat flux. The error of this treatment is visible in the early-time comparison with the Laplace reference and is small afterwards. "
  "The fifth choice is the use of a fixed evaluation horizon of 120 s, not a horizon tied to the end of the pulse train. A horizon tied to the end of the exposure would favour designs that deposit their heat late, and would not count the damage that is done while the tissue cools. A fixed horizon treats all designs alike, and the sensitivity analysis shows the effect of changing it. "
  "The sixth choice is the damage objective. The integrated burn integral is not bounded and is dominated by the interface values, while the integrated damage fraction saturates and measures a thickness of destroyed tissue; the first is used for the optimization because it has a useful gradient everywhere, and the second is reported because it has a direct physical meaning.")
P("The last choice concerns the optimizer. Nelder–Mead is a local method, and the objective has plateaus and a narrow feasible region defined by the kill constraint. The multi-start over the pulse number and the random screening before each local search address this. Each case needs of the order of five hundred to two thousand evaluations of the model, each costing a fraction of a second, so that the whole study of about sixty-five optimizations is feasible in pure Python. "
  "A gradient-based method with adjoint sensitivities would be more efficient, but it would require the derivative of a discretised model with a non-smooth maximum in the constraints, and for four design variables it would not have changed the conclusions. The independence of the results from the starting points is tested indirectly by the pulse-number loop, in which the best values for the different pulse numbers are consistent to within the spread quoted in Section 4.4.")
